(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-soap-case-presentations-quiz"]={"title":"SOAP Note and Case Presentations — Physical Diagnosis 1 Exam 3","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What do the four letters of the SOAP note stand for?","choices":["Symptoms, observations, analysis, prescription","Subjective, objective, assessment, plan","Source, onset, aggravators, palliators","Screening, orders, admission, procedure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives these four sections and calls the note a standardized way to document encounters. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the scope of a SOAP note compared with a history and physical?","choices":["More focused than a history and physical","Broader than a history and physical, since it adds the daily course","Identical in scope, differing only in the order of its sections","Longer than a history and physical, since it carries prior entries forward"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the SOAP note is more focused than an H&P while still being a standardized way to document encounters. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"The lecture summarises the subjective section as which of the following?","choices":["The measurements you recorded during the encounter","The conclusions you reached after examining the patient","The orders you intend to place before the patient leaves","What the patient said"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture subtitles the subjective section “what the patient said” and describes it as a narrative. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which part of a history and physical does the lecture say the subjective section most resembles?","choices":["The history of present illness","The review of systems","The physical examination","The identifying data"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says the subjective section is similar to the history of present illness — the story of the patient's complaint and other relevant information. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which of the following does the lecture list as belonging in the subjective section?","choices":["Vital signs recorded at the start of the visit","The results of any imaging ordered at the visit","Medications and allergies","The ranked list of potential diagnoses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The subjective section covers pertinent positives and negatives, medical and surgical history, family history, social history, review of systems, medications, and allergies. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"According to the lecture, how do you decide what counts as pertinent in the subjective section?","choices":["Anything the patient volunteers is pertinent by definition","It depends on the individual case","Only findings that appear in the chief complaint are pertinent","Pertinence is fixed by the note template for that visit type"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture answers its own question with “it depends on the individual case” and then gives categories to consider. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which category does the lecture list among the things to consider when judging pertinence?","choices":["The patient's preferred pharmacy and current insurance coverage","The length of the appointment slot the clinic has available","Information that helps rule in or rule out differential diagnoses","Whether the finding was mentioned at any previous clinic visit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists this alongside relation to the chief complaint, prior or related episodes, and risk factors. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which list matches the seven attributes of a symptom as this lecture gives them?","choices":["Site, spread of the pain, character, severity, duration, frequency, and precipitating cause","Onset, duration, severity, frequency, laterality, radiation, and the factors giving relief","Location, quality, quantity, timing, onset, palliative or provocative factors, associated manifestations","Position, pattern, precipitant, progression, prior episodes, pain score, and management plan"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. These are the seven attributes named on the subjective slide. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"In the OPPQRST mnemonic, what does the lecture say the letter O covers?","choices":["When it started, what the patient was doing, any trauma, and whether it was gradual, sudden, or chronic","Whether the pain occurs only at night, only on waking, or at no predictable hour","The organ system the clinician believes is most likely to be responsible for it","How often the pain has recurred over the past year and how long each episode ran"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture expands onset with all of these prompts. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How does the lecture qualify the severity element of OPPQRST?","choices":["Record it only when the patient volunteers a number unprompted","Record it as mild, moderate, or severe rather than as a number","Rate it from 1 to 10, and establish what a 10 means for that patient","Rate it from 1 to 5, since finer gradations are not reproducible"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture writes severity as “1-10, what is a 10?”, anchoring the scale to the individual patient. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which set of words does the lecture offer as examples of pain quality?","choices":["Mild, moderate, severe, unbearable, intolerable","Constant, intermittent, episodic, cyclical, unremitting","Anterior, posterior, medial, lateral, superficial","Sharp, dull, crushing, burning, tearing, throbbing, aching"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these as quality descriptors within OPPQRST. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does the timing element of OPPQRST capture, according to the lecture?","choices":["Whether the pain is intermittent or constant, and how it changes over time","The hour at which the patient first sought medical care","The interval between the onset of pain and the start of treatment","The number of days the patient has been away from work"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture expands timing as intermittent, constant, or changing over time. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"In the OLD ChARTS mnemonic, what do the letters C and A stand for?","choices":["Chronology and any associated illness","Character and alleviating or aggravating factors","Cause and associated abdominal findings","Comorbidities and known allergies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. OLD ChARTS runs onset, location, duration, character, alleviating and aggravating factors, radiation, temporal pattern, and symptoms associated. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What does the letter T stand for in OLD ChARTS?","choices":["Treatment already tried by the patient","Trauma preceding the onset of the symptom","Tenderness elicited on examination","Temporal pattern"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. OLD ChARTS ends with temporal pattern and then symptoms associated. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"In the ChLORIDEPP mnemonic, what does the letter I represent?","choices":["Intensity","Investigations already ordered","Impact on daily activities","Irradiation to a second site"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ChLORIDEPP runs character, location, onset, radiation, intensity, duration, extra symptoms, palliative, and provocative. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the letter E stand for in ChLORIDEPP?","choices":["Exacerbating factors","Evaluation performed at a previous visit","Extra symptoms","Emotional response to the pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture expands E as extra symptoms, sitting between duration and the palliative and provocative pair. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"In the LOCQSMAT mnemonic, what does the letter M represent?","choices":["Medications the patient takes regularly","Mechanism by which the injury occurred","Modifying factors","Maximum severity reached so far"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. LOCQSMAT runs location, onset, chronology, quality, severity, modifying factors, additional symptoms, and treatment. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which element closes the LOCQSMAT mnemonic?","choices":["Treatment","Timing","Temporal pattern","Tenderness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. LOCQSMAT ends with treatment, after additional symptoms. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How does the lecture define the content of the objective section?","choices":["The patient's own account of how the presenting problem began","Observations, measurements, and tests performed during the encounter","The diagnoses you are now prepared to commit to in writing","The instructions given to the patient on leaving the encounter"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture subtitles this section “what you found” and defines it in exactly these terms. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What guidance does the lecture give when you are unsure how far the examination should extend?","choices":["Examine every system in full, since omissions cannot be corrected later","Limit the examination strictly to the system named in the chief complaint","Defer the examination until the diagnostic testing has returned","Go a system up and down from the affected system"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives this as the rule when in doubt about how wide to examine. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which component does the lecture say the objective section always includes?","choices":["A general assessment or impression","A complete neurological examination","A rectal examination","A formal mental status score"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the objective section always includes the general assessment or impression. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which two systems does the lecture say all examinations generally include?","choices":["Neurological and musculoskeletal","Skin and lymphatic","Cardiac and respiratory","Abdominal and genitourinary"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that all examinations generally include cardiac and respiratory. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which question does the lecture pose to help decide what must be examined?","choices":["Which examination will the patient tolerate most comfortably?","What must be examined to rule in or rule out the worst-case scenario?","Which examination is quickest to document in the medical record?","Which findings are most likely to be normal on this particular patient?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs this with asking what your differentials are and how you can rule them in or out. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How many differential diagnoses does the lecture suggest aiming for?","choices":["Exactly one, so that the plan stays focused","Two, one common and one dangerous","At least ten, to demonstrate the breadth of your reading","Three or more"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says to try to have three or more. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does the lecture require of the first diagnosis listed in the differential?","choices":["It should be the most likely, explained using the history and examination","It should be the most dangerous one, whatever its actual likelihood","It should be the one the patient is most concerned about having","It should be the one that is cheapest to exclude with further testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture asks for the most likely diagnosis first, justified from the history and examination findings. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does the lecture say the differential statements should achieve for a reader?","choices":["They should list every condition capable of affecting the organ in question","They should record the exact wording the patient used when describing it","Other providers should be able to follow your thought process from them","They should establish which of the tests the insurer is willing to reimburse"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states this directly as the purpose of the differential statements. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"On what does the lecture say the assessment is based?","choices":["The chief complaint alone, since it defines the visit","The patient's stated preference among the possible diagnoses","The review of systems, which surveys every body system","The history, the physical examination, and diagnostic testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names all three, and notes the assessment uses the same format as the history and physical assessment. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Besides the final diagnosis, what else does the lecture include in the assessment?","choices":["Only those conditions newly diagnosed at this particular visit","Other conditions requiring assessment or treatment, chronic and concurrent","Only those conditions that will be treated with medication","Only those conditions the patient raised in the chief complaint"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists chronic conditions and concurrent diagnoses alongside the final diagnosis. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does disposition refer to within the plan?","choices":["The patient's attitude towards the proposed treatment","The order in which the problems are listed","Whether care will be inpatient or outpatient","The clinician responsible for signing the note"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines disposition as inpatient or outpatient and lists it first among the plan's elements. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which details does the lecture require for a pharmacologic entry in the plan?","choices":["Indications, drug name, dosage, route, and frequency","Drug class, an alternative agent, and the expected cost","Prescription number, dispensing pharmacy, and refill count","Drug name only, since dosing is set by the pharmacist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists all five for the pharmacologic component of treatment. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"}]};
